#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186093403 :tutorial/content "We know that we can write a system of three linear equations in three variables in the form $A\\mathbf{x}=\\mathbf{b},$ where\n\n- $A$ is the $3\\times 3$ coefficient matrix,\n\n- $\\mathbf{x}$ is the column vector of variables, and\n\n- $\\mathbf{b}$ is the column vector of constants.\n\nIf the $3\\times 3$ matrix $A$ is invertible, then we can find a solution to the system $A\\mathbf{x} = \\mathbf{b}$ by \"pre-multiplying\" both sides of the equation by $A^{-1}{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Ax & =b \\\\ {A}^{-1}Ax & ={A}^{-1}b \\\\ {I}_{3}x & ={A}^{-1}b \\\\ x & ={A}^{-1}b\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, to find the solution of $A\\mathbf{x}=\\mathbf{b},$ we simply calculate $\\mathbf{x}= A^{-1}\\mathbf{b}.$\n\nLet's see how this works for a particular system in the next example." :tutorial/id #uuid "4c0fe954-2cf5-5eb6-b58f-c6963f5be7f2" :tutorial/math-academy-id 3054 :tutorial/title "Introduction"}]}
